# The place at infinity of a Weierstrass function field

Theorem statement: `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.FunctionField.eq_valuationSubring_of_X_not_mem`
(`bff6a520-dc9f-5b24-835e-8bcc33de64d9`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `4641f4d0-4caa-5cb4-8802-9b2a4c39f633`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field and $W$ a Weierstrass curve over $K$, with affine coordinate ring $K[W] =
(K[X])[Y]/(W\text{-polynomial})$ and function field $K(W)$, the fraction field of $K[W]$. Let $O$ be
a valuation subring of $K(W)$ such that (i) the image of every constant $c \in K$ under the
structure map $K \to K(W)$ lies in $O$, and (ii) the image in $K(W)$ of the class of $\mathrm{C}\,X
\in (K[X])[Y]$ — that is, the coordinate function $x$ — does not lie in $O$. Let $v$ be a valuation
on $K(W)$ with values in $\mathbb{Z} \cup \{0\}$ written multiplicatively as
$\mathrm{WithZero}(\mathrm{Multiplicative}\,\mathbb{Z})$, satisfying $v(f) = \exp\bigl(\deg
N(f)\bigr)$ for every nonzero $f \in K[W]$, where $N = \mathrm{Algebra.norm}$ for the extension
$K[W]/K[X]$ and $\deg$ is the degree of the resulting polynomial in $K[X]$. Then $O$ coincides with
the valuation subring $\{g \in K(W) : v(g) \le 1\}$ of $v$.

This identifies the unique place of the function field of a Weierstrass curve at which the
coordinate function $x$ has a pole, namely the place of the point at infinity, characterised here as
the valuation subring of the degree-of-norm valuation; together with the description of the places
containing $x$ it classifies the places of $K(W)$ trivial on $K$. It is used in the construction of
places attached to points and in establishing that divisors of degree zero on a Weierstrass curve
over an algebraically closed field are principal.

## Explanation of the source proof

